```

The inverse guard keeps signed-in users out of the sign-in/sign-up group:

```tsx
// app/(auth)/_layout.tsx
import { useAuth } from '@clerk/expo'
import { Redirect, Stack } from 'expo-router'

export default function AuthLayout() {
  const { isLoaded, isSignedIn } = useAuth()

  if (!isLoaded) return null
  if (isSignedIn) return <Redirect href="/(home)" />

  return <Stack />
}
```

Expo Router group names in parentheses don’t become URL segments. Put these hook-based guards in child group layouts, not the root layout that initializes `ClerkProvider`. These redirects are client-side route protection; sensitive data still needs server-side authorization.  
Source: [Clerk’s Expo Router guide](https://clerk.com/articles/how-to-set-up-clerk-authentication-with-expo-router).

### Version and peer-dependency fit for the requested target

The current registry entry reports **`@clerk/expo@4.14.1`** with these relevant peer ranges:

- `expo`: `>=54 <59` — includes Expo SDK 58.
- `react`: `^18.0.0 || ^19.0.0` — includes React 19.3.
- `react-native`: `>=0.75` — includes React Native 0.88.
- `react-dom`: `^18.0.0 || ^19.0.0` — optional peer, generally not needed for a native-only app.

It also lists Expo peers for optional feature integrations: `expo-crypto >=12`, `expo-constants >=12`, `expo-web-browser >=12.5.0`, `expo-auth-session >=5`, and `expo-secure-store >=12.4.0`; additional optional peers cover passkeys, biometrics, Google/Apple sign-in, and native components. The registry metadata makes the broad SDK 58 / RN 0.88 / React 19.3 version ranges compatible; use `npx expo install` to resolve Expo-package versions for the project.  
Source: [npm registry metadata for `@clerk/expo@4.14.1`](https://registry.npmjs.org/@clerk%2Fexpo/4.14.1).

**Caveat:** Clerk’s compatibility article I read documents Expo SDKs 54–55, not SDK 58. The SDK 58 compatibility conclusion above comes from the package’s live peer-dependency ranges, rather than an SDK-58-specific Clerk compatibility table.  
Source: [Clerk compatibility article](https://clerk.com/articles/clerk-compatibility-in-expo-54-and-55)."}]}
{"kind":"agent","id":"eHqAHt4fQ0o","agentType":"researcher-web","displayName":"Web Researcher","prompt":"Research the CURRENT Supabase third-party auth integration with Clerk (for a React Native client using @supabase/supabase-js v2), from https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/third-party/clerk and https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/signing-in-with-oauth (only if needed). Report: exact Supabase dashboard steps to add Clerk as third-party auth (audience, allowed client URLs, JWKS URL), whether the RLS subject claim is `auth.jwt()->>'sub'`, the recommended supabase-js client setup on mobile where the Clerk access token must be fetched at request time (is there a third-party access token strategy, how to get a Clerk JWT access token from @clerk/clerk-expo — e.g. session.getToken()), any resolution vs raw-bearing JWT config, and any migration requirements for existing-era tables like `profiles`. Include exact code snippets and URLs you read.","status":"done","blocks":[{"kind":"tool","id":"eHqUjlCgwo8","toolName":"web_search","input":{"query":"site:supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/third-party/clerk Clerk Supabase third party auth dashboard audience allowed client URLs JWKS URL React Native accessToken","depth":"standard"}},{"kind":"tool","id":"eHqUjo5di1I","toolName":"web_search","input":{"query":"site:clerk.com/docs @clerk/clerk-expo session.getToken Supabase third party access token strategy supabase-js","depth":"standard"}},{"kind":"tool","id":"eHqUjxVdwUc","toolName":"web_search","input":{"query":"site:supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/signing-in-with-oauth Supabase [… 9,970 characters omitted]  polyfill and an optional AsyncStorage configuration. That storage is for Supabase Auth sessions; using Clerk as the external token provider does not mean you should sign into Supabase Auth separately. ([Supabase: JavaScript initializing](https://supabase.com/docs/reference/javascript/initializing))

### Don’t configure a JWT template for the native integration

Clerk’s older Supabase JWT-template integration is deprecated; Clerk and Supabase recommend the native third-party integration. The native approach uses Clerk session tokens, so don’t pass a Supabase JWT-template name to `getToken()` for this setup and don’t share your Supabase signing secret with Clerk. ([Supabase: Clerk guide](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/third-party/clerk); [Clerk: Supabase integration](https://clerk.com/docs/guides/development/integrations/databases/supabase))

### Existing `profiles` tables

**No blanket migration is required** just because you add Clerk: Supabase says third-party auth can be used without migrating users to Supabase Auth. But existing schema assumptions may need adjustment. ([Supabase: third-party auth overview](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/third-party/overview))

In particular, check whether `profiles.id` is a UUID foreign key to `auth.users`, and whether a trigger creates profile rows from `auth.users`. Those are Supabase Auth-specific patterns; Clerk-authenticated users are not thereby inserted into `auth.users`. Supabase’s profile example explicitly uses `id uuid references auth.users`. ([Supabase: managing user data](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/managing-user-data))

A low-disruption option is to keep an internal UUID primary key and add a text Clerk ID for ownership:

```sql
alter table public.profiles
  add column clerk_user_id text unique;

alter table public.profiles enable row level security;

create policy "Users can read their own profile"
on public.profiles
for select
to authenticated
using (clerk_user_id = (select auth.jwt()->>'sub'));
```

Populate `clerk_user_id` for existing profiles using a reliable mapping to Clerk users. If your current RLS policies compare UUID IDs with `auth.uid()`, update them to use the Clerk text subject (or the new text column). Don’t convert UUID identifiers or remove foreign keys/triggers without first checking how the application uses them."}]}
